/// Recursion is bounded, and the bound belongs to the language.
///
/// A tree-walking interpreter recurses on the host's stack, a native binary on
/// the machine's, a wasm module on the engine's. Three stacks, three sizes, so
/// "how deep may a program go" used to have three answers: at depth 30,000 the
/// native binary printed 30000 while the reference semantics died with a Rust
/// runtime message, exit 127, no `file:line` and nothing a Vyrn program could
/// have caught. That is the one thing this project promises never happens.
///
/// So the depth is COUNTED, not left to whichever stack runs out first: 10,000
/// calls may be in flight, and the 10,001st ends the run with the same words in
/// every engine. An `extern` does not count (it is the host's frame) and neither
/// does a lambda body (it has no name to call itself by, so it cannot recurse
/// without passing through a named function like this one).
///
/// The trap is last, because it ends the run — this example is a three-way
/// parity citizen through its stdout, its stderr and its exit code, like the
/// other trapping examples.
fn down(n: Int64) -> Int64 {
    if n <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return 1 + down(n - 1)
}

fn main() -> Int64 {
    // Exactly at the budget: `main` holds one frame and `down` takes 9,999 more.
    print("\{down(9998)}")
    // One past it. The run ends here.
    print("\{down(9999)}")
    return 0
}
